\section{What this needs, and what happens without it}{

The individual votes are not in the per-accession cache -- they never were,
they were summarised and discarded. \code{evaluate_reference_accessions()}
began persisting them to a sidecar \code{reference_pair_cache.rds} on
2026-09-02. An accession evaluated before that date, or with
\code{cache_dir = NULL}, has no pair rows, so it CANNOT be refined: it keeps
its original verdict, \code{trust_refined} reads \code{FALSE}, and a message says how
many accessions that applied to. Re-running
\code{evaluate_reference_accessions()} on those accessions is what builds their
pair rows; there is no way to reconstruct them without a fresh BLAST.
}

\section{Determinism}{

Every iteration updates every accession simultaneously from the PREVIOUS
iteration's confidences (a Jacobi sweep, not Gauss-Seidel), so the result
does not depend on the order accessions appear in. The \code{top_n} slice
breaks percent-identity ties by \code{id_y} rather than by input order, for the
same reason.
}

\section{What it did on real data (2026-09-02)}{

On the PtConception 12S screen the motivating case was \verb{Askoldia variegata} (\code{MT627596}), the disagreeing partner in 4 of the 12
\code{"incongruent"} verdicts. Its own row reads
\code{"insufficient_independent_evidence"} with a 100\% agreeing hit and nothing
disagreeing -- \code{label_confidence} 0.999. So the honest answer is that
weighting does not discount it: it is an under-evaluated partner, not a
likely error, and the disagreement it registers is a real family-level
distinction in a thinly-covered clade. The mechanism is right and the
cascade guard is doing its job; this particular accession was simply not
the culprit it looked like.
}

